{"kind":"propose","text":"Charter for the GitHub connector on generalbusiness-ai/gitseq. Offered for ratification because nothing may append under it until it is ratified, and because two of its clauses are hugh's to decide rather than mine.\n\nSCOPE. The connector watches issues on github.com/generalbusiness-ai/gitseq, a public repository with issues enabled and, as of now, none filed. It binds to this workroom, genesis 5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8. It watches issues and issue comments only; pull requests are the outbound half and are chartered separately.\n\nACTOR. A single rostered actor of kind `service`, named `github-connector`, holding one key. Foreign principals are carried as data in the act body, never as separate identities: the connector attests \"I observed that foo@bar filed this\". Admitting that actor is itself a roster grant and needs ratification; this charter proposes it rather than presuming it.\n\nMAPPING. An issue becomes one durable statement carrying `source: github`, `external_id: \u003cowner/repo#number\u003e`, and `on_behalf_of: \u003cgithub login\u003e`, resting on this charter event. Issue comments are observed the same way, resting additionally on the statement for their issue. Nothing else is durable: labels, reactions, edits and closes are rendering inputs, not acts. Idempotency uses the connector's own namespace with the GitHub delivery identifier as the key, so at-least-once redelivery collapses to a replay rather than a second act.\n\nDOORSTEP — the first thing that is yours. Two defensible answers, and I recommend the second.\n\n(a) Anyone with a GitHub account may cause a durable act. Simplest, and matches \"public front-door\" most directly. It also means any passer-by can append to your permanent log, and the only remedy after the fact is supersession by someone with authority.\n\n(b) Anyone may file an issue and be observed, but what the observation becomes depends on who filed it. Issues from accounts bound by a ratified alias, or from repository collaborators, may become `request` statements that assign work. Issues from everyone else become `assert` statements — durable, attributed, visible, and carrying no obligation on anybody until a member picks them up and files the request themselves.\n\nI recommend (b). It keeps the front door genuinely open, since anything filed is recorded and readable, while preserving the property that a commitment exists only because someone in the room accepted it. Under (a) a stranger can create work assignments in your log; under (b) a stranger can create evidence, which is what an issue actually is.\n\nGITHUB CREDENTIAL — the second thing that is yours, and I flag it because it is not in the design note. The only GitHub credential on this machine is your personal token, scoped `repo`. If the connector uses it, everything the connector does on GitHub appears as hughpyle. Inbound that is merely inaccurate; outbound, when it opens pull requests and edits comments, it is the same attribution theatre the design refuses on the gitseq side, only pointing the other way. The connector should have its own GitHub identity — a machine account or a GitHub App installation — before it writes anything. I would rather build inbound against your token as read-only and block outbound on a real connector identity than quietly ship something that signs your name.\n\nDELEGATED AUTHORITY. None. The connector may state observations and, under (b), file requests. It may not ratify, may not approve, and may not merge.\n\nWHAT THIS CHARTER DOES NOT DO. It is detection, not prevention. The pre-append hook checks only that the submitting key is on the static allowlist; the fold does not read charter bodies and does not know what a charter is. An allowlisted connector whose key is stolen can state anything a connector may state, and this charter will not stop it — it gives attribution and a flare afterwards, not a refusal at the door. Whether that should change is an open question in the design note and is not settled here."}